{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1958-mirror-final",
  "Source": "P. Baumeister, \"Design of multilayer filters by successive approximations,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 48, 955-958 (1958). doi:10.1364/JOSA.48.000955 - Table II (p. 957), column 'Final design'",
  "Notes": "Broad-band dielectric mirror after application of the refining program to the starting design.\nSpecification: Desideratum: nearly uniform reflectance of 94 +/- 1 % from 400 to 700 nm; the final result does not meet it.\nReported: Text p. 957: final result (solid line, Fig. 3) does not meet the specification. Fig. 3 (approximate readings): R ~0.90-0.92 at 400 nm, maxima ~0.97-0.98 near 440, 540 and 620 nm, minima ~0.93-0.94 near 480, 590 and 660 nm, falling steeply above ~680 nm to ~0.8 at 700 nm.\nNotes: Table II lists films from the substrate (layer 1, n = 2.30, next to the 1.52 substrate) to layer 15 (n = 2.30) next to air; reversed here so layer 1 = paper's layer 15. Each value is the wavelength (nm) at which the film is a quarter wave; physical thickness = lambda_q/(4 n), rounded to 0.0001 nm, with n = 2.30 (odd) and 1.35 (even), non-absorbing. Normal incidence assumed. Massive substrate (no back surface). No total thickness printed. reference_nm 667 is only nominal.\nLayers from Table II (p. 957), column 'Final design'; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
